WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.259
Film Courage: Can you think back to one of
your first scripts and when you look at now

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:09.000
where you are and having critiqued people’s
work and rewritten it and all of the things

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.320
that you’ve done in your career, what was
wrong with your first script?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.839
Jill Chamberlain, Script Consultant/Author/Writer:
I had the chronic problem that is the whole

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:23.650
reason I created my method, the whole thing
that is behind The Nutshell Technique.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:29.400
So everybody thinks they have a great idea
for a movie, right?

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.060
They don’t.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:36.710
What you might have is the great idea for
one, you don’t have a story though?

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.690
You have what I would call a situation.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:44.960
And I would identify that in my work as a
script consultant 99% of the time that’s

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.120
what writers are writing.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.530
They are failing to write a story.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.420
Instead they are creating a situation.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.160
Life is a situation, right?

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.120
Life is…this happens, then this happens,
then this happens, then this happens.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.440
That’s not a story.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.550
Story is this happens which leads to that
happening which makes it ironic when this

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:08.550
other thing happens.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:12.040
There’s this connection between the parts.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.940
That’s the problem I’m seeing in 99% of
writers that they are creating a situation

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.960
not a story and that was exactly my problem.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:28.940
I thought I had a great idea (this would make
a great movie) and I couldn’t understand

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.990
why I was feeling so blocked and why I was
having a hard time writing it.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:34.990
I thought it was me.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.950
I thought I must not be a good enough writer
or I must not be talented enough.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.310
No, the problem was…well that might be true
too…but the other problem was I didn’t

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.260
have technique basically and technique and
technique to understand the difference between

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.340
what a situation is and what a story is.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:57.270
If you just have a situation you are going
to be laboring to get from moment to moment

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.740
to moment and there’s not going to be a
natural connection and a natural progression

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.970
and so it’s going to feel stilted, it’s
going to feel unnatural.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.500
It’s going to feel like bad writing.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:17.350
But with technique, that’s the secret to
making something feel like an organic story

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.780
that has resonance and has power.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:25.140
It’s not just reading somebody’s diary
of unconnected events, these different events

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.840
that happen to somebody.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:33.069
There’s that glue that is existing behind
them that makes them coherent.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.780
So I absolutely suffered from the same problem
that I’ve been addressing, that’s the

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.180
whole reason I created my method.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:43.280
Film Courage: Do you remember where you were
or what was going on when you had that ‘aha’

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.910
moment about this?

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.430
Were you watching something?

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:55.690
Jill: I was getting kind of a similar note
to the one I described that people were saying

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:00.080
things like (they were using different words)
but they were saying essentially this isn’t

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.880
really a story, it’s a situation.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.900
The only thing was no one could explain to
me the difference.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.060
I had a sense of what the difference was.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.900
I had good training.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.520
I had studied at Columbia University.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:21.230
I’d also studied with some private screenwriting
programs in New York City and learned a lot

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:27.720
of good things in these different programs
but no one was bringing it all together and

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.290
I could sense in this note, I knew there was
truth in what I was hearing even though no

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.170
one could explain to me how to transform it.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:44.110
I got what they were saying and I had a sense
that there are (if I’m going to make a huge

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:47.650
generalization) two approaches out there.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:53.840
One is a linear approach, a strictly three-act
structure, beat method kind of approach to

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.840
screenwriting.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.970
And the other is a more character based, character
arc approach.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:07.120
The problem I was having (and the problem
that 99% of writers are having) I could tell

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.599
belonged somewhere in the interaction between
these methods.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:19.279
That these methods do not exist, if a story
is functioning properly, these are not separate

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:20.279
things.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.800
Character arc in three act story structure.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.689
They are actually very specific points where
they need to intersect and if you fail to

00:04:28.689 --> 00:04:34.089
hit those intersections you are going to end
up with a situation but not a story.

00:04:34.089 --> 00:04:40.600
So with this note I was getting, I started
analyzing movies, I started deconstructing

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.639
movies.

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:48.409
And I deconstructed hundreds of them, just
kind of obsessively taking notes and trying

00:04:48.409 --> 00:04:50.620
to find these moments.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:58.009
It took a little while and right off the bat
I could identify essentially eight elements

00:04:58.009 --> 00:05:04.300
that are required to tell the story and my
method and these are interconnected elements

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:10.439
these are not beats, these are not moments
in time, these are elements of the story.

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:15.349
I’d say right off of the bat I knew what
five of them were but it took a little while

00:05:15.349 --> 00:05:18.419
of analyzing movies and then it did happen.

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:23.979
It was kind of this breakthrough moment on
the last movie that I deconstructed in this

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:25.520
period where I was like “Boom!

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.520
The Eight Method.”

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.520
And I was like “Oh my gosh.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.520
This actually works.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.520
I can’t believe it?”

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.039
Film Courage: What movie was it?

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:32.370
I’m just curious.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:36.150
Jill: You know…I don’t know that I can
identify the movie.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.219
I had notebooks full of them at that time.

00:05:38.219 --> 00:05:43.789
But it was kind of like Holy Cow…this actually
works once that last one dropped.

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:48.330
And then I could use those eight things and
apply it against every movie from then on.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.259
I was like “Oh my gosh, they’re there,
they’re there.

00:05:50.259 --> 00:05:52.419
I can’t believe it’s there in every single
movie.”

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:55.680
Film Courage: Well you go into several movies
in your book [The Nutshell Technique]?

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.680
Jill: Yes, I do.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.580
Film Courage: From CHINATOWN to SILVER LININGS
PLAYBOOK, you run the gamut.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.430
Jill: Yes, I made a point of doing that.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:03.430
So the book…I’ll show you the back.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.430
Film Courage: Please hold it up.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:05.430
Beautiful cover by the way.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:06.430
Jill: Yes, thank you.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.740
So it’s a nice lovely large format book
and I recommend people get the paperback not

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:16.309
the kindle for that reason because it’s
just a beautifully designed large format book

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:21.930
and one of the big features of the book is
the last section I have in it 30 different

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:23.710
movies.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:28.910
So it’s all in my one nutshell form (one
form), 30 different movies.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:33.129
And so the book is designed for you to flip
back and forth when you’re reading.

00:06:33.129 --> 00:06:40.189
I mention a movie so you can look and feel
how it fits in the alphabetical listing of

00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:45.659
movies in the back you can see how that element
fits into the whole picture.

00:06:45.659 --> 00:06:54.460
I have everything in the back from CASABLANCA
to PULP FICTION to AUGUST: OSAGE COUNTY.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:07:00.240
Movies that are different time periods, different
genres, movies that you probably think almost

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.960
had nothing in common structure wise otherwise
maybe 3-acts (maybe).

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.599
I can identify these three elements in all
of these different ones.

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:14.479
So I just wanted to illustrate that this is
not formulaic, it’s not a formula, right?

00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:21.669
We are talking about very, very different
movies that are not following one template.

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:27.969
No one would say that the template to PULP
FICTION and CASABLANCA are anything alike.

00:07:27.969 --> 00:07:34.159
But underlying it at the very basis they have
the same structural that we are looking at

00:07:34.159 --> 00:07:41.309
that are required for making them the great
movies that they are, they are the same right

00:07:41.309 --> 00:07:42.449
things going on.

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:47.819
Film Courage: And you can send an email if
someone would like a graph?

00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:56.159
Jill: Yes, so my nutshell form, the central
tenet of my method is my Nutshell Technique

00:07:56.159 --> 00:07:57.159
form.

00:07:57.159 --> 00:08:01.139
Basically I drew up these eight elements that
are required to tell a story and put it on

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:07.259
a one-page form for my clients and my students
and I rather glibly wrote on the first one

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:12.339
“Screenplay In A Nutshell” and that’s
how the name was born.

00:08:12.339 --> 00:08:18.770
With this form, it’s a visual method, we
can see right on the piece of paper what’s

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.969
working and what is not working.

00:08:21.969 --> 00:08:23.529
Anyone can download it.

00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:26.099
My website is Jill Chamberlain (dot) com.

00:08:26.099 --> 00:08:30.340
You can download free versions of these worksheets
for your own use.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:34.900
You can take the Nutshell Technique and start
playing around with it using it for your own

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:35.900
stories.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.660
What’s important (I want to emphasize) is
that it’s not just…it’s different than

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.330
the beat sheets methods.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:44.150
These are not beats in time.

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:47.380
Four of these are moments in time.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:52.250
What’s really different is the inner connection
between the parts.

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:58.270
I don’t know any other method in the 3,000
books on screenwriting that are on Amazon,

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:05.000
none of the ones that I’m aware of explains
the connection to between the parts.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.870
Most of the books that you find on Amazon
that deal with structure they do what I call

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:15.150
a beat sheet method where they tell you you
are supposed to hit these pre-prescribed fifteen

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:20.610
or twenty two or however many beats that happen
in a typical Hollywood.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:23.450
I’m throwing all of that out.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:29.610
These are not moments in time other than a
first scene, a last scene, identifying things

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:30.770
like that.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:34.620
These are other structural elements to the
story other than moments in time.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.860
What’s important is the connection between
the parts, the glue that is going to hold

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:39.860
them together.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:43.830
Film Courage: I’m wondering what a movie
looks like when it’s just a bunch of situations

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:45.880
strung together?

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:46.880
How does this look?

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.550
Let’s suppose a screenwriter has done this,
they haven’t fixed [their script], now the

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:51.750
film is out there.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.570
What does that look like?

00:09:54.570 --> 00:10:00.110
Jill: It feels episodic, that’s another
way to put it, where it’s kind of one moment

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:01.110
after moment.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:08.010
It’s almost like we’re seeing separate
short films strung together, instead of one

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:09.320
coherent story.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:12.670
And by the way, not every movie tells a story.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:15.820
I’m talking about movies that tell a story.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:22.650
Actually some of the films Richard Linklater
famously (and he has said this quite a bit)

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:28.380
that movies like SLACKER and BOYHOOD are great
movies, they’re not telling a story though

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:29.380
right?

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:32.180
It’s not one coherent story.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.140
It’s looking at a slice of life, it’s
looking at different…it’s following a

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:36.140
character.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:43.270
There might be some things thematically pulling
them in common but it’s not a story.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:47.130
So not every movie tells a story.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.850
I have to say that it’s extremely rate to
find a movie in theaters that doesn’t tell

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:52.850
a story.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:11:01.370
It’s pretty unusual that Richard Linklater
is the exception to the rule where we see

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:06.440
something that is successful that we can be
moved by that actually isn’t telling a story.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:10.820
The vast majority of the time movies need
to tell a story for us to feel satisfied.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:11.820
We’re going to get restless.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:13.390
It’s going to feel episodic.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:15.570
We’re going to get lost on tangents.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:17.500
Why are we following this character?

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.350
What did that moment have to do with that
moment?

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.620
There are going to be things that happen where
you are setting plants but you’re not paying

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:25.620
off.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:32.910
And it’s a chronic problem that I see in
screenwriters that they are failing to tell

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:33.420
a story.

